  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1213 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nQuiet weather will prevail again today as gusty southerly winds \ncontinue to advect warmer air into the region this afternoon. Expect \nhigh temperatures to climb a few degrees higher than yesterday, \nreaching into the lower 70s.\n\nAnother weak shortwave will ripple through the Central Plains this \nafternoon, resulting in the development of a few showers. While the \nmajority of precipitation will likely struggle to make it through a \ndry sub-cloud layer, a few locations over southeast Nebraska and \nsouthwest Iowa could see some light rain or sprinkles this evening.\n\nWednesday through Friday...\n\nUpper level ridging shifts eastward over the central CONUS by \nmidweek, keeping highs about 10 degrees above normal, in the mid to \nupper 70s Wednesday through Friday.\n\nA strong trough will push through the intermountain west on Friday, \ntightening the pressure gradient, and causing southwesterly winds to \nsurge across the Central Plains. Gusts up to 30-40+ mph can be \nexpected Friday afternoon through Saturday morning, before a cold \nfront brings winds around to the northwest. Expect increased fire \ndanger Friday afternoon, especially with any dry standing crops \nwaiting to be harvested. Thankfully, increasing humidity with the \napproaching trough should help mitigate extreme fire behavior to \nsome extent. \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nTemperatures will begin to fall behind the front on Saturday. Expect \nhighs to range from the mid 60s in northeast Nebraska to the mid 70s \nin far southeast Nebraska/southwest Iowa, Saturday afternoon. \n\nShowers will move in with the frontal passage Saturday.  Expect rain \nchances to increase through Saturday and continue into Sunday \nafternoon, with the greatest chance for rain remaining over \nnortheast Nebraska (40-60%). \n\nLatest model runs look to briefly cut off the upper low from the \nmainstream flow, over the Four Corners region Saturday evening. This \ncutoff low eventually ejects northeastward early next week, merging \nback into the upper level flow. This would allow additional showers \nto develop Monday into Tuesday (15-30%).  \n\nTemperatures dip closer to normal as we head into next week, with \nhighs forecast to generally top out in the mid 50s to low 60s Sunday \nthrough Tuesday.",
